          %h2#idea Idea
          %blockquote
            %p I can illustrate the second approach with the same image of a nut to be opened. The first analogy which came to my mind is of immersing the nut in some softening liquid, and why not simply water? From time to time you rub so the liquid penetrates better, and otherwise you let time pass. The shell becomes more flexible through weeks and months – when the time is ripe, hand pressure is enough, the shell opens like a perfectly ripened avocado!
            %p A different image came to me a few weeks ago. The unknown thing to be known appeared to me as some stretch of earth or hard marl, resisting penetration… the sea advances insensibly in silence, nothing seems to happen, nothing moves, the water is so far off you hardly hear it… yet it finally surrounds the resistant substance.
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"}> Alexander Grothendieck
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/R%C3%A9coltes+et+semailles"} Récoltes et semailles
              = succeed "\u201D)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/The+Rising+Sea"} The Rising Sea
            %p I don’t want you to think all this is theory for the sake of it, or rather for the sake of itself. It’s theory for the sake of other theory.
            %p
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> J. Lurie
              ,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://gowers.wordpress.com/2010/08/31/icm2010-spielman-csornyei-lurie/"} ICM 2010
          %p
            The tools of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            serve to organize other structures. There is a plethora of applications that have proven to be much more transparent when employing the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} nPOV
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p The following is a (incomplete) list of examples of topics for which higher category theory has proven to be useful.
          %h3#in_geometry In geometry
          %p
            The field of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            has long managed to avoid the change to an
            = succeed "-point" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometric
            structures in the physics of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            (such as that of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientifold"} orientifold
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gerbe"} gerbe
            = succeed "s)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal ∞-bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+and+topos+theory"} sheaf and topos theoretic
            concepts, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+space"} diffeological space
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+stack"} differentiable stack
          %p
            For instance the ordinary category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transversal+map"} transversal map
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            in the form of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
          %p
            In all notions of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+space"} generalized smooth space
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+stack"} derived stack
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+smooth+manifold"} derived smooth manifold
          %p
            Recent developments in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Structured+Spaces"} Structured Spaces
            based on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            that realizes old ideas about how category theory provides a language for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+and+quantity"} space and quantity
            in great detail and powerful generality and sheds new light on old
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical
            problems such the description of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A+Survey+of+Elliptic+Cohomology+-+the+derived+moduli+stack+of+derived+elliptic+curves"} derived moduli stack of derived elliptic curves
            and the construction of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tmf"} tmf
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            from it. This construction has benefited tremendously from the adoption of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} nPOV
          %h4#DiffEqu In differential equations
          %p
            Much of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+space"} topological vector space
            theory, e.g., the theory of
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distribution"} distribution
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nuclear+space"} nuclear space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+differential+equation"} partial differential equation
            theory and is intensely conceptual (categorical) in spirit. It is routine these days to accept distributional solutions, but it wasn’t always so, and it was the efficacy of the abstract TVS theory which changed people’s minds.
          %p
            Way back Cartan studied differential equations in terms of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exterior+differential+system"} exterior differential system
            = succeed "POV," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+%E2%88%9E-algebroid"} Lie ∞-algebroid
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+Lie+algebroid"} tangent Lie algebroid
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"} Bill Lawvere
            noticed in the 1960s that the notion of differential equation makes sense in any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+topos"} smooth topos
            (as described
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/differential+equation#InSynthDiff"} here
            %em Categorical dynamics
            he promoted the point of view that all things
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometric
            can be formulated in abstract category theory internal to a suitable
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+space"} infinitesimal
          %p
            Closely related to both these perspectives, a modern point of view on differential equations that is proving to be very fruitful regards them as part of the theory of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-module"} D-module
          %h3#in_cohomology In cohomology
          %p
            A multitude of notions of cohomology and its variants are unified from the
            = succeed "POV" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+hom+space"} ∞-categorical hom-spaces
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topoi"} (∞,1)-topoi
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
          %h4#hochschild_cohomology Hochschild (co)homology
          %p
            Specifically, the subject of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+cohomology"} Hochschild cohomology
            %em higher order Hochschild cohomology
            effectively merges into the canonical concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-powering"} (∞,1)-powering
            of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+cohomology"} Hochschild cohomology
            for details.
          %h3#InHomotopyTheory In homotopy theory
          %p
            The study of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            originated in the study of categories such as those of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed "es" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            was introduced in order to deal with structures “up to homotopy”. But it was clear that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            captured only a very small part of the interesting information. Quillen introduced the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            as a formalization of the full structure, and this formalization turned out to yield a powerful theory that today provides a powerful toolset for dealing with homotopy theoretic situations.
          %p
            But also the notion of model category was seen to not be the full answer. For instance a model category in a sense retains
            %em too much
            non-intrinsic information. Equivalence classes of model categories under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
            are a more intrinsic characterization of a given
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher categorical
            notion for the collection of all model categories. This problem came to be known as the search for the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong homotopy theory of homotopy theories
          %p
            Recently, this problem was fully solved and homotopy theory fully understood as the special case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            that deals with
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-categories
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the notion of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+model+category"} simplicial model category
                serves as a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} presentation
                of the notion of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            %li
              %p
                the “homotopy theory of homotopy theories” is accordingly the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-category of (∞,1)-categories
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi Cat
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)Cat
              %p
                better yet: there is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category"} (∞,2)-category
                of all
                = succeed "-categories;" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %li
              %p
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)Cat
                two
                = succeed "-categories" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                = succeed "s;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
            %li
              %p
                all “homotopy”-constructions in model category theory, such as
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"} homotopy limit
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+cone"} mapping cone
                %em tools for constructing
                the corresponding higher categorical intrinsic notions, such as
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+in+a+quasi-category"} limit in an (∞,1)-category
            %li
              %p
                all variant notions find their intrinsic higher categorical interpretation this way: for instance
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
                is the study of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-categories
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
                of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
                is simply the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decategorification"} decategorification
                of the corresponding
                = succeed "-category" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-category"} 1-category
            %li
              %p
                and for instance the notion of homotopy category of a stable
                = succeed "-category" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
                into the picture.
          %h4#RationalHomotopyTheory In rational homotopy theory
          %p
            … The study of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+theory"} rational homotopy theory
            is naturally understood as the study of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization+of+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} localizations
            of
            = succeed "es" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            with certain line-object coefficients. See
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+theory+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} rational homotopy theory in an (∞,1)-topos
          %h3#in_ktheory In K-theory
          %p
            In full generality, (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+K-theory"}> algebraic
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            is a universal assignment of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectra"} spectra
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} stable (∞,1)-categories
          %p …
          %h3#in_tannaka_duality In Tannaka duality
          %p
            … see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality"} Tannaka duality
            …
          %h3#in_differential_geometry In differential geometry
          %p See at
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry+applied+to+plain+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry applied to plain differential geometry
          %h3#InDifferentialCohomology In differential cohomology
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
            solves the
            %a{:href => "differential%20cohomology%20diagram#SimonsSullivan07"} Simons-Sullivan question
            characterization on the characterization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod-type)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology+hexagon"} differential cohomology hexagon
            for details.
          %h3#DeformationTheory In deformation theory
          %p
            In deformation theory it was early on recognized that for a good theory the notion of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+differential"} Kähler differential
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotangent+complex"} cotangent complex
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A+Survey+of+Elliptic+Cohomology+-+the+derived+moduli+stack+of+derived+elliptic+curves"} derived moduli space of derived elliptic curves
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E-ring"} E-∞-ring
          %p
            It turns out that all these concepts are special cases of a construction obtained from a simple higher categorical notion, that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} tangent (∞,1)-category
          %h3#Logic In logic and type theory
          %p
            While it is common to view logic as the study of absolute truth, in fact logic can have many different interpretations, or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantics
            %em categorical semantics
            for logic and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %a{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory#CategoricalSemantics"} internal language
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
          %p
            In particular, the internal logic of a category (such as a topos) is, in general,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            (and also stronger statements, such as the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %em not
            be true in the internal logic of many interesting categories. Conversely, category-theoretic models have provided new insight into the independence of various axioms in constructive mathematics, such as differing forms of the axiom of choice.
          %p
            As another example, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            in Martin-Löf’s original constructive dependent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            construct, from any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and terms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b \in A
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Id
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id_A(a, b)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+types"} propositions as types
            interpretation, the elements of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Id
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id_A(a,b)
            are proofs that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            are propositionally equal; thus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Id
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id_A(a,b)
            is a replacement for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+value"} truth value
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a=b)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi Id
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to Id(a, a)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Id
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Id
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Id
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id(b, c) \times Id(a, b) \to Id(a, c)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Id
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Id
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id(a, b) \to Id(b, a)
            expressing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} reflexivity, transitivity and symmetry
            of this propositional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Id
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id(a,a)
            %em isomorphisms
            in a
            = succeed "\u2014or," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            theory and
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
            for more details.
          %p
            This is now known as
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %h3#in_probability_theory In probability theory
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category-theoretic+approaches+to+probability+theory"} category-theoretic approaches to probability theory
          %h3#Physics In physics
          %p
            See also
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory+and+physics"} higher category theory and physics
          %h4#ClassMech Classical mechanics and its geometric quantization
          %p
            By the end of the 19th century a fairly complete, powerful and elegant mathematical formulation of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            of symplectic geometries.
          %p
            But there were some loose ends. Notably the fully general theory involved
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            was realized to be more naturally described by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multisymplectic+geometry"} multisymplectic geometry
          %p
            Both these generalizations have a natural common higher categorical formulation: that of
            = succeed "s:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+%E2%88%9E-algebroid"} Lie ∞-algebroid
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroid"} Poisson Lie algebroid
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-symplectic+manifold"} n-symplectic manifold
          %p
            Moreover, the quantization step of geometric quantization was understood to be effectively the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of these
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+%E2%88%9E-algebroid"} Lie ∞-algebroid
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} Lie ∞-groupoid
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %h4#QuantumMechanics Quantum mechanics and quantum information
          %p
            The basic structure of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
            is encoded in the theory of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger-compact+categories"} dagger-compact categories
          %p …
          %h4#GaugeTheory Gauge theory
          %p
            Maxwell realized that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            is controlled by a degree 2-cocycle in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham cohomology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            that characterize a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            on a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
          %p
            Later the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+field"} Yang-Mills field
            was understood to similarly be a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection on a bundle
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            some possibly nonabelian
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
          %p
            While thinking about the mathematical structures possibly underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
            or the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
          %h5#Supergravity Supergravity
          %p
            Theories of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            have been known to require higher
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%27Auria-Fre+formulation+of+supergravity"} D'Auria-Fre formulation of supergravity
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal ∞-bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            some super
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} Lie ∞-group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E-algebra"} L-∞-algebra
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+Lie+3-algebra"} supergravity Lie 3-algebra
          %h4#BVBRST BV-BRST formalism
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+formalism"} BV-BRST formalism
            is secretly a way to talk about the fact that configuration spaces of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theories
            are not naive spaces such as
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
          %p
            the configuration space is really an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Conf
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi Sh
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi dgAlg
                    %mo −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Conf \in Sh_{(\infty,1)}((dgAlg^-)^{op})
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stack"} ∞-stack
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-site"} (∞,1)-site
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi dgAlg
                    %mo −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (dgAlg^-)^{op}
            of certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} ∞-algebras
            modeled as
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+geometry"} derived
            function algebra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Conf
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi dgAlg
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{O}(Conf) \in dgAlg
                  \\,.
          %blockquote
            %p (many more aspects go here, eventually)…
          %h4#QFT Quantum field theory
          %p
            There are essentially two axiomatizations of what
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            is, both of which are inherently
            = succeed "-categorical:" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
                picture – the
                %em Schrödinger picture
                – a quantum field theory is described as an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-functor"} (∞,n)-functor
                on an
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category+of+cobordisms"} (∞,n)-category of cobordisms
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
                – now a theorem that characterizes central properties of these
                = succeed "," do
